post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'

==> tpmtb: TPM Trust Bundle
    Verify that your TPM was manufactured by a trusted vendor by checking its
    Endorsement Key (EK) certificate against manufacturer CA certificates.

    This bundle is unrelated to the system TLS trust store (ca-certificates);
    it only establishes trust in TPM hardware identity, not network connections.

  1. Download the verified trust bundle (integrity + provenance checked via Rekor):

       tpmtb bundle download

     This writes tpm-ca-certificates.pem and tpm-intermediate-ca-certificates.pem
     to the current directory.

  2. Extract your TPM's EK certificate (requires tpm2-tools):

       tpm2_getekcertificate -o ek.pem

     This reads the cert from the TPM's NV storage. If not found, retrieval
     is vendor-specific — see man tpm2_getekcertificate for details.

  3. Verify the EK cert against the manufacturer bundle:

       openssl verify \
         -CAfile tpm-ca-certificates.pem \
         -untrusted tpm-intermediate-ca-certificates.pem \
         ek.pem

     "ek.pem: OK" means the EK cert chains to a trusted manufacturer root.
     Note: some EK certs contain TPM-specific critical extensions that openssl
     may reject even for a valid chain. Use the Go SDK's VerifyCertificate for
     programmatic verification — it handles these extensions correctly.

  4. (Optional) Verify the bundle's own provenance at any time:

       tpmtb bundle verify tpm-ca-certificates.pem

  See: https://github.com/loicsikidi/tpm-ca-certificates

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}
